  "full_response": "Consensus algorithms are fundamental in distributed systems, ensuring that multiple nodes (servers) agree on a single value or state, even in the presence of failures. **Raft** is a consensus algorithm designed to be understandable and practical, addressing the same problem as **Paxos** but with a simpler and more intuitive approach. Below is an explanation of how Raft works:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Core Goals of Raft**\nRaft ensures that a distributed system achieves **consensus** while maintaining:\n- **Safety**: No two nodes will ever disagree on the state (e.g., the log of commands).\n- **Availability**: The system remains operational as long as a majority of nodes are alive.\n- **Fault Tolerance**: The system can tolerate up to `f` failures in a cluster of `2f + 1` nodes.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Key Concepts in Raft**\n#### **a) Roles of Nodes**\nIn Raft, each node can be in one of three states:\n1. **Leader**: Handles all client requests, replicates logs to followers, and manages commit decisions.\n2. **Follower**: Passively receives log entries from the leader and responds to its requests.\n3. **Candidate**: A temporary state during leader election (when no leader exists).\n\n#### **b) Terms (Logical Clocks)**\n- Raft divides time into **terms** (numbered sequentially, e.g., Term 1, Term 2).\n- Each term begins with a **leader election**.\n- Terms act as a logical clock to detect stale leaders or candidates.\n\n#### **c) Log Replication**\n- The leader appends client commands to its log and replicates them to followers.\n- Once a majority of nodes have stored the log entry, it is **committed** (applied to the state machine).\n- Followers only apply committed entries to their state machines.\n\n#### **d) Leader Election**\n- If followers don’t hear from a leader for a **randomized election timeout**, they transition to **candidate** and request votes.\n- A candidate becomes leader if it receives votes from a **majority of nodes**.\n- Terms ensure that only one leader can exist per term (preventing \"split-brain\").\n\n---\n\n### **3. How Raft Works Step-by-Step**\n#### **Step 1: Leader Election**\n1. All nodes start as **followers**.\n2. If a follower doesn’t receive a heartbeat from the leader within its **election timeout** (random",
